        "text": "### Summary\n\nOnboarding a new build server currently takes 3 to 4 working days of manual work; the steps live in a shared chat thread and in the heads of two engineers, and every new server needs a person to walk through the setup step by step before it can run the first build.\n"
      },
      "question_1": {
        "text": "### What Is the Problem?\n\nProvisioning and configuring a new build server is a manual, undocumented process.\n"
      },
      "question_2": {
        "text": "### Why Is It a Problem?\n\nEvery new server blocks project kickoffs until a senior engineer is available to drive the setup, and knowledge loss has already caused two broken handovers.\n"
      },
      "question_3": {
        "text": "### Where Is the Problem Observed?\n\nIn the DevOps build environment, during every build-server addition.\n"
      },
      "question_4": {
        "text": "### Who Is Impacted?\n\nEngineering managers planning project start dates and the build teams waiting for usable infrastructure.\n"
      },
      "question_5": {
        "text": "### When Was the Problem First Observed?\n\nDuring the first build-server handover in March 2026.\n"
      },
      "question_6": {
        "text": "### How Is the Problem Observed?\n\nLate project kickoffs and post-handover incidents that trace back to missing setup steps.\n"
      },
      "question_7": {
        "text": "### How Often Is the Problem Observed?\n\nOnce per new build server, which the current plan puts at one to two servers per quarter.\n"
      },
      "text": "Current State"
    },
    "gap": {
      "text": "## Gap\n\nA new build server takes 3 to 4 working days to onboard, while the expected duration is half a day of largely automated setup.\n"
    },
    "impact": {
      "text": "## Impact\n\nThe delay costs the team approximately 1500 EUR per quarter in rework and lost schedule buffer.\n"
    },
    "future_state": {
      "text": "## Future State\n\nA new build server is provisioned, configured, and verified by an automated runbook in under half a day, without a senior engineer driving the setup.\n"
